    "lawCode": "Amd §8, Chap 592 of 1998",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8044A\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the Hudson river park act, in relation\nto permitting the construction of an art installation within the bounda-\nries of pier 52\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo allow the Whitney Museum to install and maintain \"Day's End\", a\npublic artwork by David Hammons, to stand on the exact site where Pier\n52 once stood in the Hudson River.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends the Hudson river park act, adding a new paragraph (d)\nto allow the Whitney Museum to construct, donate to the Hudson River\nPark Trust, maintain, and repair an art installation entitled \"Day's\nEnd\" by David Hammons.\n\nSection 2: This act shall take effect immediately\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTo enable this worthy project to proceed, this very specific authori-\nzation is required.\n\nIn 1975, Gordon Matta-Clark made a series of cuts in the walls of the\nPier 52 shed in the Hudson River Park, transforming the shed into a \"sun\nand water temple\" which he named \"Day's End.\" The shed at Pier 52, along\nwith Matta-Clark's work, has since been demolished. In homage to Matta-\nClark's work and the history of New York City, David Hammons' sculpture\nby the same name will consist of a life-size skeletal replica of the\nPier 52 shed where it once stood. This permanent public art installation\nwill be constructed and maintained by the Whitney Museum, and owned by\nthe Hudson River Park Trust. The sculpture may not be materially\naltered, sold, leased, exchanged, donated, subjected to concession\nagreement, or used for other than its original purpose without the\nexpress authority of an act of the legislature.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
